<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    NphiesCore launch screen.

    WHAT IT DRAWS. One full-bleed image view showing the `Splash` image set from
    Assets.xcassets — the NphiesCore mark centred on the brand plate, with a
    light and a dark variant so the launch screen matches the device appearance
    instead of flashing the wrong plate for a frame.

    THIS FILE IS SHOWN TWICE PER COLD START, WHICH IS WHY IT MUST STAY TRIVIAL.
    iOS renders it before any app code runs, and then @capacitor/splash-screen
    re-instantiates THE SAME STORYBOARD as its own splash view — it reads
    UILaunchStoryboardName out of Info.plist and calls
    instantiateInitialViewController (SplashScreenPlugin/SplashScreen.swift:91).
    So the pre-launch frame and the post-launch splash are the same pixels, and
    there is no seam between them. Break that by adding a second scene, an
    outlet, or anything needing a custom class, and both phases break at once —
    a launch storyboard gets no code, so an outlet is a crash before main().

    contentMode is scaleAspectFill ON PURPOSE. The source is a 2732x2732 square
    and every device it lands on is taller or wider than square, so aspectFill
    always covers the screen and crops the plate margins, which carry nothing.
    aspectFit would draw the mark at true proportion but letterbox with the
    root view's background — and a storyboard cannot express an appearance-
    adaptive colour inline, so one of light or dark would get bands in the
    wrong plate. Covering is the only option that is correct in both.

    THE BACKGROUND COLOUR IS THE DEGRADED STATE, not normally visible. It was
    systemBackgroundColor, which is pure white in light mode: if the `Splash`
    asset is ever renamed, dropped or fails to compile, the launch screen went
    to a white rectangle — the blank-launch failure this comment exists to
    prevent. It is now the dark brand plate #101519, the same value as
    `ios.backgroundColor` and `plugins.SplashScreen.backgroundColor` in the root
    capacitor.config.json. Those three agree deliberately; the plugin overwrites
    this view's backgroundColor with its own configured value
    (SplashScreen.swift:43), so a disagreement would show up as a colour change
    partway through the launch.

    THE ASSET ITSELF IS NOT GENERATED BY scripts/make-icons.sh. That script
    rebuilds every raster from public/icon*.svg but writes no splash, so the six
    PNGs in Assets.xcassets/Splash.imageset are whatever @capacitor/assets last
    produced from assets/splash*.png. Check them against the current mark before
    an upload — a wordless brand change is exactly the kind of thing that ships.
-->
<document type="com.apple.InterfaceBuilder3.CocoaTouch.Storyboard.XIB" version="3.0" toolsVersion="17132" targetRuntime="iOS.CocoaTouch" propertyAccessControl="none" useAutolayout="YES" launchScreen="YES" useTraitCollections="YES" useSafeAreas="YES" colorMatched="YES" initialViewController="01J-lp-oVM">
    <device id="retina4_7" orientation="portrait"/>
    <dependencies>
        <deployment identifier="iOS"/>
        <plugIn identifier="com.apple.InterfaceBuilder.IBCocoaTouchPlugin" version="17105"/>
        <capability name="documents saved in the Xcode 8 format" minToolsVersion="8.0"/>
    </dependencies>
    <scenes>
        <!--View Controller-->
        <scene sceneID="EHf-IW-A2E">
            <objects>
                <viewController id="01J-lp-oVM" sceneMemberID="viewController">
                    <imageView key="view" userInteractionEnabled="NO" contentMode="scaleAspectFill" horizontalHuggingPriority="251" verticalHuggingPriority="251" image="Splash" id="snD-IY-ifK">
                        <rect key="frame" x="0.0" y="0.0" width="375" height="667"/>
                        <autoresizingMask key="autoresizingMask" widthSizable="YES" heightSizable="YES"/>
                        <color key="backgroundColor" red="0.062745098039215685" green="0.082352941176470587" blue="0.098039215686274508" alpha="1" colorSpace="custom" customColorSpace="sRGB"/>
                    </imageView>
                </viewController>
                <placeholder placeholderIdentifier="IBFirstResponder" id="iYj-Kq-Ea1" userLabel="First Responder" sceneMemberID="firstResponder"/>
            </objects>
            <point key="canvasLocation" x="53" y="375"/>
        </scene>
    </scenes>
    <resources>
        <image name="Splash" width="2732" height="2732"/>
    </resources>
</document>
